%S A400640 3,5,10,53,1525,1306361,344954577372,7016304653840719074152,
%T A400640 31820730852033156792052128012301576216850
%N A400640 Number of equivalence classes of monotone Boolean functions of n variables under cyclic rotations of the variables.
%C A400640 Two monotone Boolean functions are equivalent if one is obtained from the other by a cyclic permutation of the variables.
%H A400640 Bartlomiej Pawelski, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2108.13997">On the number of inequivalent monotone Boolean functions of 8 variables</a>, arXiv:2108.13997 [math.CO], 2021. See p. 2 (Table 2).
%H A400640 Andrzej Szepietowski, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.03868">Fixes of permutations acting on monotone Boolean functions</a>, arXiv:2205.03868 [math.CO], 2022.
%F A400640 Let Fix(pi) be the number of monotone Boolean functions of n variables fixed by the permutation pi of the variables, as studied by Pawelski and Szepietowski.
%F A400640 a(n) = (1/n) * Sum_{k=0..n-1} Fix(rho^k), where rho = (1,2,...,n).
%F A400640 Equivalently, a(n) = (1/n) * Sum_{d|n} phi(d) * Fix(d^(n/d)), where Fix(d^(n/d)) denotes Fix(pi) for a permutation pi of cycle type d^(n/d).
%e A400640 For n = 6, the numbers Fix(rho^k), k = 0,...,5, are 7828354, 44, 562, 8600, 562, 44. Hence a(6) = (7828354 + 2*44 + 2*562 + 8600)/6 = 1306361.
%Y A400640 Cf. A000372 (all monotone Boolean functions), A003182 (equivalence under arbitrary permutations of the variables).
%K A400640 nonn,hard,more,new
%O A400640 1,1
